{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CE5H14o5LhUdq1Eiug5Kxr","type":"tool_result","content":"**Title:** \"Re-visiting the O/Cu(111) system -- When metastable surface oxides could become an issue!\"\n\n**Authors:** Norina A. Richter, Chang-Eun Kim, Catherine Stampfl, Aloysius Soon\n\nI can't reproduce the full abstract verbatim because it exceeds the 125-character limit on quoted material. Here is a summary in my own words:\n\nThe paper examines whether a metastable copper oxide surface structure, called the \"8\"-structure, forms on Cu(111). Earlier X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy and low-energy electron diffraction experiments had seen this structure after exposing the surface to a hyperthermal oxygen beam. Using density-functional theory, ab initio atomistic thermodynamics, and Boltzmann statistics, the authors find the structure is metastable, and that its population is comparable to two known oxide films on Cu(111). They also find that its atomic arrangement in the top two layers resembles Cu₂O(110), a facet proposed as highly active for water splitting under light. The authors suggest the structure could be reactive if it formed during catalysis.\n\nThe full abstract is available on the arXiv abstract page (arxiv.org/abs/1411.0276)."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9d545003-cc6e-4da5-a676-71f1c32de4a0","uuid":"dc138880-1f42-43ce-a18f-35c9d96ddf23","timestamp":"2026-11-01T07:36:57.922Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":45268,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"**Title:** \"Re-visiting the O/Cu(111) system -- When metastable surface oxides could become an issue!\"\n\n**Authors:** Norina A. Richter, Chang-Eun Kim, Catherine Stampfl, Aloysius Soon\n\nI can't reproduce the full abstract verbatim because it exceeds the 125-character limit on quoted material. Here is a summary in my own words:\n\nThe paper examines whether a metastable copper oxide surface structure, called the \"8\"-structure, forms on Cu(111). Earlier X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy and low-energy electron diffraction experiments had seen this structure after exposing the surface to a hyperthermal oxygen beam. Using density-functional theory, ab initio atomistic thermodynamics, and Boltzmann statistics, the authors find the structure is metastable, and that its population is comparable to two known oxide films on Cu(111). They also find that its atomic arrangement in the top two layers resembles Cu₂O(110), a facet proposed as highly active for water splitting under light. The authors suggest the structure could be reactive if it formed during catalysis.\n\nThe full abstract is available on the arXiv abstract page (arxiv.org/abs/1411.0276).","durationMs":3665,"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1411.0276"},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01CE5H14o5LhUdq1Eiug5Kxr","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01JB2KaMSr7G5AFFvFdBXM2z","type":"tool_result","content":"**Title:** \"Mechanistic Insights into the Early Stages of Oxidation at Copper Terrace:\" \"The Role of O-O Repulsion and Substrate-mediated Effects\"\n\nThe full title exceeds the 125-character quote limit, so I've split it into two quotes.\n\n**Authors:** \"E V Charan Reddy\" and \"Abhijit Chatterjee\"\n\n**Abstract:** I can't reproduce the abstract verbatim because it's longer than the 125-character limit for quotes. In summary, the paper uses density functional theory to show that subsurface copper oxide can form directly on Cu(111) terraces. This happens through clusters of adsorbed oxygen atoms that pull copper atoms out of the surface layer, opening channels for oxygen to diffuse below. The subsurface oxide forms without an energy barrier, which suggests the copper oxide surface is highly dynamic. At low oxygen coverage, however, subsurface oxygen is unstable, and substrate-mediated O-Cu-O interactions govern oxide stability.\n\nYou can read the full abstract at arxiv.org/abs/2505.12914."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9d545003-cc6e-4da5-a676-71f1c32de4a0","uuid":"92e51399-2cdd-4c54-b424-a38139dfe3fa","timestamp":"2026-11-01T07:37:00.690Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":43329,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"**Title:** \"Mechanistic Insights into the Early Stages of Oxidation at Copper Terrace:\" \"The Role of O-O Repulsion and Substrate-mediated Effects\"\n\nThe full title exceeds the 125-character quote limit, so I've split it into two quotes.\n\n**Authors:** \"E V Charan Reddy\" and \"Abhijit Chatterjee\"\n\n**Abstract:** I can't reproduce the abstract verbatim because it's longer than the 125-character limit for quotes. In summary, the paper uses density functional theory to show that subsurface copper oxide can form directly on Cu(111) terraces. This happens through clusters of adsorbed oxygen atoms that pull copper atoms out of the surface layer, opening channels for oxygen to diffuse below. The subsurface oxide forms without an energy barrier, which suggests the copper oxide surface is highly dynamic. At low oxygen coverage, however, subsurface oxygen is unstable, and substrate-mediated O-Cu-O interactions govern oxide stability.\n\nYou can read the full abstract at arxiv.org/abs/2505.12914.","durationMs":7061,"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.12914"},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01JB2KaMSr7G5AFFvFdBXM2z","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
